__registerContent("newspaper/society/2026-07-24/education-collapse", {"notes":{"news-2026-07-24-education-collapse":{"newsType":true,"date":"24 July 2026","paper":"The Hindu","subject":"Society / GS-2 (Education, Governance)","summary":"An opinion article by Sonia Gandhi (Chairperson, Congress Parliamentary Party) arguing that three structural crises underlie the student protests triggered by NEET paper leaks: (1) the Modi government's retreat from public education funding, (2) the gutting of the public examination system through centralisation via NTA, and (3) the difficulty of succeeding in India's education system amid high unemployment.","keyPoints":[{"heading":"Context: Student Protests","points":["Protests triggered by NEET-UG paper leaks and demands for NTA reform and resignation of Education Minister Dharmendra Pradhan","On 20 July 2026: Delhi Police and CAPFs used lathi charges and tear gas on protesters at Jantar Mantar","152 paper leaks across India in the past 12 years, including 9 by NTA since it was formed in 2017"]},{"heading":"Issue 1: Retreat from Public Education","points":["Government reduced proportion of total Budget spent on school education by 50% and on higher education by 33%","In 12 years: nearly one lakh public schools shut down while 43,000 private schools opened (many run by RSS-affiliated organisations)","UGC cut grants to universities, pushed them to take high-interest loans → universities raised fees","Families in India spend enormous sums out of pocket: NEET coaching alone equals total public education investment in many states","India's higher education system: bound to obsolete systems, politically motivated curricular changes, unable to keep pace with AI-enabled world"]},{"heading":"Issue 2: Centralised Examination System via NTA","points":["NTA (National Testing Agency): set up by Union Cabinet WITHOUT parliamentary deliberations; no accountability mechanism","Traditional practice: universities and states setting their own examinations replaced by centralised NTA-conducted exams (NEET, CUET, JEE)","NTA described as: hapless, chronically-understaffed, relies on army of private contractors; paper-setters not from premier institutions","NEP 2020 cleared without seeking consensus or subjecting it to parliamentary deliberations","Education Minister dismissed Parliamentary Standing Committee recommendations; refused to resign despite massive failures"]},{"heading":"Issue 3: Employment Crisis","points":["High-quality, well-paid jobs after graduation: rare today","Youth unemployment averaging ~40% in the last few years","Growing monopolisation of the economy","Students investing enormously in education but facing bleak job prospects"]},{"heading":"Cross-links","points":["GS-2 Education Policy: NEP 2020, NTA, CUET, Right to Education (RTE) Act","GS-2 Governance: Parliamentary oversight of executive bodies, accountability mechanisms, Cabinet vs Parliament","GS-3 Economy: Youth unemployment, skill development, education-employment mismatch","GS-4 Ethics: Use of state force on peaceful protesters, accountability of public officials","Essay: 'India's demographic dividend — potential or peril?'"]}],"importantTerms":[{"term":"NTA (National Testing Agency)","explanation":"Established by the Union Cabinet in 2017 as an autonomous body to conduct entrance examinations for higher educational institutions. Conducts: NEET-UG (medical), JEE (engineering), CUET (central universities), UGC-NET. Controversy: 152 paper leaks in 12 years across India; NTA responsible for 9 of these since 2017. The CJI-appointed panel in 2024 reviewed its functioning after NEET 2024 controversy."},{"term":"NEP 2020 (National Education Policy)","explanation":"India's overhauled education policy replacing NPE 1986. Key features: 5+3+3+4 school structure, foundational literacy focus (NIPUN Bharat), multiple entry-exit in higher education, Academic Bank of Credits, multidisciplinary approach. Cleared by Cabinet in July 2020 without parliamentary vote. Critics argue it was not debated in Parliament; proponents argue it is the most comprehensive education reform since independence. It is a CENTRAL government policy."},{"term":"CUET (Common University Entrance Test)","explanation":"Introduced from 2022-23 as the centralised entrance test for admissions to central universities, replacing individual university entrance exams. Conducted by NTA. Aims: uniform admission process. Criticism: overburdening students, technical glitches, reduces diversity in admissions."},{"term":"UGC (University Grants Commission)","explanation":"Statutory body established under UGC Act, 1956 under the Ministry of Education. Coordinates, determines, and maintains standards of university education. Distributes grants to universities. Recent controversies: granting recognition to deemed universities, changing PhD norms, fee regulation disputes."}],"mainsAnswerNotes":["The NEET controversy exposes a deeper structural failure in India's education governance: the tension between centralisation (for uniformity) and decentralisation (for contextual relevance). The NTA was created by executive fiat without parliamentary debate — a pattern replicated in NEP 2020 — weakening democratic accountability in education policymaking.","The fiscal retreat from public education (school spending down 50%, higher education down 33%) combined with fee hikes in public universities has created a two-tier system: well-resourced private institutions for the affluent, under-resourced public institutions for the rest. This contradicts constitutional mandates (Article 21A, RTE) and India's aspiration to harness its demographic dividend.","Youth unemployment averaging ~40% reveals an education-employment mismatch: India is producing graduates whose skills don't match market needs, partly because curricula are designed by politically appointed bodies rather than industry-academic partnerships.","The use of force on student protesters raises GS-4 and GS-2 concerns: Article 19(1)(b) guarantees the right to peaceful assembly; the government's response must be proportionate and lawful. Police accountability requires independent oversight — a structural gap in India's governance architecture."],"prelims":["NTA set up in 2017 by Union Cabinet (not Parliament); conducts NEET, JEE, CUET, UGC-NET","NEP 2020: cleared July 2020 by Cabinet; replaced NPE 1986; 5+3+3+4 structure","CUET: introduced from 2022-23; centralised entrance for central universities","152 paper leaks in India in 12 years; 9 by NTA since 2017","UGC: statutory body under UGC Act 1956; under Ministry of Education","Article 21A: Right to Education (RTE) — free and compulsory education 6-14 years"]}},"quizzes":{"news-2026-07-24-education-collapse":{"title":"Education System Collapse (24 Jul 2026)","questions":[{"q":"The National Testing Agency (NTA) was established in which year and by which authority?","options":["2014 by an Act of Parliament","2017 by Union Cabinet (without parliamentary vote)","2020 under NEP 2020 by Ministry of Education","2022 by a Supreme Court order"],"correct":1,"explain":"NTA was established in 2017 by the Union Cabinet as an autonomous body — it was NOT created through parliamentary legislation, which is a key point of constitutional accountability critique."},{"q":"The article claims India's higher education budget was reduced by what percentage in the last 12 years?","options":["20%","33%","50%","66%"],"correct":1,"explain":"According to the article, the proportion of the total Budget spent on higher education was reduced by 33% (school education was reduced by 50%)."},{"q":"NEP 2020 replaced which earlier National Education Policy?","options":["NPE 1968","NPE 1986","NPE 1992","NPE 2005"],"correct":1,"explain":"The National Education Policy 2020 replaced the National Policy on Education 1986 (with its 1992 modification) — the previous major education policy framework."},{"q":"Assertion (A): The centralisation of entrance examinations under the NTA has reduced accountability in India's education system.\nReason (R): The NTA was set up by executive order without parliamentary debate, lacks adequate staffing, and has been associated with 9 paper leaks since 2017.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true and R correctly explains A — the combination of executive creation (bypassing Parliament), understaffing, reliance on private contractors, and 9 paper leaks are precisely the mechanisms through which accountability has been weakened."},{"q":"CUET (Common University Entrance Test) was introduced from which academic year?","options":["2020-21","2021-22","2022-23","2023-24"],"correct":2,"explain":"CUET was introduced from the 2022-23 academic year as the centralised entrance test for admissions to central universities, conducted by NTA."},{"q":"Which fundamental right is most directly engaged when police use tear gas and lathicharges to disperse student protesters?","options":["Article 14: Right to Equality","Article 19(1)(b): Right to Assemble Peacefully","Article 21: Right to Life and Personal Liberty","Article 32: Right to Constitutional Remedies"],"correct":1,"explain":"Article 19(1)(b) guarantees the right to assemble peacefully without arms — this is the fundamental right most directly engaged when authorities use force to disperse peaceful protests."}]}}});
